menu "BSH D-Bus"

choice BSH_BOARD
    prompt "Adapter board"
    default BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6
    default BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3
    default BSH_BOARD_CUSTOM
    help
      Selects the default pin mapping. Choose Custom to set pins by hand.

config BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6
    bool "Bouni BSH-Board (ESP32-C6)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6

config BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3
    bool "kiu BaSHi (ESP32-C3)"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C3

config BSH_BOARD_CUSTOM
    bool "Custom"

endchoice

config BSH_PIN_RX
    int "D-Bus RX pin"
    default 4 if BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6
    default 10 if BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3
    default -1
    help
      GPIO receiving from the DATA wire (via the open-drain level shifter).

config BSH_PIN_TX
    int "D-Bus TX pin (configured but never written)"
    default 5 if BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6
    default 9 if BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3
    default -1
    help
      GPIO wired to the DATA line driver. This firmware is strictly read-only
      and never transmits; the pin is handed to the UART driver only to match
      the established ESPHome configuration for these boards.

      On the Bouni board this is safe in every state: the driver (U1) is a
      74LVC1G07 open-drain buffer and R14 pulls the net to 3.3V, so the bus is
      released whenever the pin is high -- including while it floats during
      reset and boot. Nothing in this firmware ever writes to it.

config BSH_PIN_LED_STATUS
    int "Status LED pin (-1 to disable)"
    default 6 if BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6
    default 8 if BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3
    default -1

config BSH_PIN_LED_ACTIVITY
    int "Activity LED pin (-1 to disable)"
    default 7 if BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6
    default -1

config BSH_PIN_BOOT
    int "BOOT button pin (-1 to disable the recovery hold)"
    default -1 if BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3
    default 9
    help
      Holding this button for five seconds during normal operation erases the
      stored WiFi credentials and reboots, which brings the setup access point
      back. It is the only way back into a device that joined a network you
      cannot reach.

      On the C6 this is GPIO9, which is also the strapping pin: holding it while
      resetting puts the chip into download mode instead. That is a separate,
      deliberate function -- press it only after the board is already running.

      Disabled on the kiu BaSHi, and not by oversight. GPIO9 is the C3's boot
      pin, and the BaSHi wires that same pin to the level shifter as the bus TX
      line. The button on the module shorts it to ground, so holding it would
      pull DATA low for as long as it is held -- this firmware would be
      disturbing the bus it promises never to touch. The way back into a BaSHi
      is a cable.

config BSH_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "LEDs are active low"
    default y if BSH_BOARD_BOUNI_C6
    # The BaSHi's own logger sketch drives its status LED with LED_ON == LOW.
    default y if BSH_BOARD_KIU_C3
    default n

config BSH_BUS_DEFAULT_BAUD
    int "Bus baud rate (0 = detect automatically)"
    default 0
    help
      0 listens on 9600, 19200 and 38400 in turn at startup and keeps whichever
      rate yields CRC-valid frames. Costs a few seconds once per boot and
      removes a whole class of confusing failures, since a wrong rate looks like
      a broken parser rather than a wrong setting.

      Set a fixed rate once you know it. Most appliances use 9600; the COM1
      module is documented to probe upwards, so newer ones may be faster.

endmenu
